WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Hey Crazies.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.960
This mirror reflects all visible light,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.740
but so does this piece of paper.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.380
What’s the difference? Why aren’t they the same?

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.260
I actually get this question a lot in the comments.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.020
Let’s see where it takes us.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.140
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.540
Alight, so the quick answer is that there's two different kinds of reflection.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.300
The mirror sends the light in a very specific direction.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.060
We call this specular reflection

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.980
because “specular” means “mirror” in Latin.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.840
With this type of reflection, you see a different image depending how you look at the mirror.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.020
The image changes as you move around a room.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.980
It gives you the illusion you’re looking through a window into some mirror universe.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.340
Paper is a little different.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.460
With paper, the light scatters instead.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.880
It spreads out, so we call it diffuse reflection.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.740
If you move around the room, you’ll keep seeing the same thing:

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:00.760
White.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.580
The word reflection is pretty fitting for both types though.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.780
It comes from the Latin “reflectere” meaning “to bend backward,”

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.820
which is exactly what both types of reflection do.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.300
They bend the light backward, but in very different ways.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.280
They’re going to end being the same, aren’t they?

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.340
Yep. You’re finally learning.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.420
Ok, so you’re probably wondering how that’s possible.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.220
How could these two different phenomena be the same?

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.880
Well, the usual explanation goes something like this.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.920
A mirror is incredibly smooth, even under a microscope,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.160
so the surface always faces the same direction.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.640
But, with something like paper, the surface has tiny imperfections.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.720
The light still obeys the law of reflection at each separate spot,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.760
but different spots face different directions, so the light scatters instead.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.420
It sounds pretty convincing,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.700
except it can’t be the whole story.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.320
If it were, a rough piece silver would look white

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.900
and it doesn’t.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.200
But it’s definitely part of the explanation.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.260
I mean, all of us can tell this smooth piece of plastic is not a mirror,

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.380
but it definitely has some mirror-like effects.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.220
It’s just not the whole explanation.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.620
There must be something else going on here.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.100
Hmm.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.940
We’re going to have to go deeper,

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.400
like, literally deeper into the material.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.660
Super zoom!

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.760
The mirrored surface is made of atoms, usually silver or some other shiny metal.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.960
We like to imagine that light interacts with the atoms on top,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.020
but there are spaces between those atoms that the light can fit through.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:42.120
Scattering can happen off many layers of atoms and still contribute to the reflected light.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.460
The mirror-like reflection in this plastic is from the surface atoms.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.240
The color is from the layers underneath.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.460
And that’s not even considering the wave nature of light.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.600
If we do that, each of those atoms sends out its own light in all directions,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.960
including into the material.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.860
Ultimately, those light waves cancel except in two specific directions.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.200
You heard me right.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.000
The incoming beam splits in two at any surface.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.240
It doesn’t have to be transparent.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.240
Some of the light will reflect.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.440
We call that the reflected light for obvious reasons.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.620
The rest will move forward into the new material.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.320
We call that the transmitted or refracted light.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.820
How much is reflected or transmitted depends on lots of things:

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:32.400
The materials involved, the incoming angle, the frequency of the light, etc.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.920
For an air-glass boundary, the light is about 5% reflected and 95% transmitted,

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.520
because transparency.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.720
For a mirrored surface, it’s the other way around.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.660
The point is there’s always some transmitted light,

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.060
even for a mirror.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.360
What makes these materials different is how far that transmitted light can go.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.760
It’s something we call Penetration Depth.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.800
[Boing Noises]

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.960
This penetration depth depends on four things:

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.980
The electric and magnetic field properties of the material, the frequency of the incoming light,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.660
and, most importantly, the electrical resistance the material can provide.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.620
The three material properties are just numbers you look up in a table,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.000
like this one from Engineering Toolbox.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.220
As always, links in the doobly-doo.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.500
I calculated a couple of these depths just so you can get some perspective.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.200
The penetration depth for glass is 156.5 meters.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.720
That’s over 500 feet.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.320
For a silver mirror, it’s 1.4 nanometers.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.980
That’s drastically different from glass.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.200
So it goes that far and just stops?

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.920
Well, no.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.180
The light intensity drops off exponentially.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.040
A penetration depth of 1.4 nanometers means that,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.900
for every 1.4 nanometers the light goes into the silver,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.979
the intensity goes down by a factor of e.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:59.000
At one penetration depth, the intensity is 37% of the original.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.520
At five penetration depths, the intensity drops below 1%.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.420
For the silver mirror, that’s still only 7 nanometers.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.580
That transmitted light is going nowhere fast.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:15.980
Based on this equation, we can see transmitted light won’t go as far in electrical conductors.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.440
That’s what gives metals that distinctive metal coloring we all recognize.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.600
The light barely penetrates.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.720
So, why aren’t all insulators transparent?

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.740
Oh. Uh. Uh. Whoops!

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.400
There’s one more issue we have to resolve.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.540
These atoms need energy to send out their own light.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.280
It doesn’t come out of nowhere.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.520
Energy must be conserved.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.460
Conservation of energy shall not be violated!

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.480
That incoming light is absorbed and used,

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.340
but there’s a catch.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.560
There are two different ways that light can be absorbed by those atoms:

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.140
Resonant Absorption and Dissipative Absorption.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.980
I know. I know. Those are some serious 10-dollar words,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.600
but they’re not as fancy as they sound.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.360
Resonant absorption is when a single electron absorbs a single photon

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.080
with just the right energy needed to bump the electron to a higher energy level.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.760
This is the kind of absorption that results in things like fluorescence and phosphorescence.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.660
This is not the kind of absorption that creates reflected and transmitted light beams.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:19.860
I repeat.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.380
Resonant absorption does not create reflected and transmitted light beams.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.880
When this happens, the electron usually doesn’t have a chance to re-emit that light.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.500
It gets stolen by neighboring atoms as heat instead.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.360
This is the light that heats up a material.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.120
Fluorescence and phosphorescence are just special exceptions.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.260
Reflected and transmitted beams come from the other type of absorption:

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.140
Dissipative Absorption.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:50.360
This type doesn’t require quantum mechanics. It is a very classical effect.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.520
An atom is just minding its own business when some light comes along.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:59.960
Because the atom is made of charge, the light’s electric field drives the atom’s motion.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.600
The atom vibrates at the same frequency as the light.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.420
Vibration is a kind of acceleration and accelerated charges emit light.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:12.040
This vibration causes the atom to send out its own light,

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.520
which combines with the light from other atoms.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:20.500
How all that light combines depends on things like atomic spacing, response lags,

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.640
vibrational damping and resonances.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.000
That’s too many things for our brains to handle,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:30.800
so we take that overwhelming amount of information and summarize it as a single unitless number:

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.740
the refractive index.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.760
That’s another number we can just look up in a table somewhere.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.240
If we have that number, we can forget atoms and molecules even exist.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.260
All we need to know is the type of material and we’re good to go.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:50.120
Just keep in mind, this refractive index depends slightly on the frequency of the incoming light.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:54.340
Different kinds of light can transmit or reflect better than others.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.780
That’s what gives everything their color.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:08:00.180
Even off of a mirror, green reflects better than other colors,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.140
which is why, if you look deep into mirror infinity, things tend to start turning green.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.199
So why isn’t everything a mirror?

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.440
Well, a few reasons.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.380
First, the surface has to be really smooth.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.340
Too many imperfections and the light will scatter.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.780
Second, the surface has to be a good electrical conductor.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.720
That way the light barely penetrates.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.480
Silver is the best, but it’s also expensive,

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.200
so a lot of mirrors are made of aluminum instead.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.800
Lastly, the light shining on the surface can’t match the quantum energy gaps of the electrons,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:36.920
otherwise the light is destined to become heat rather than a reflection.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.920
If you check all those boxes, you’ve got yourself a mirror.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.440
If not, it’ll just be a solid color.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.940
So, got any more questions about mirrors?

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.560
Please ask in the comments.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.320
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.820
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.660
And until next time, remember, it’s ok to be a little crazy.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.580
Several of you asked if anyone had ever done diffraction off of a mirror.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:01.760
Oh yeah, for sure.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.260
I actually had to do it as a lab in Modern Physics in undergrad.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.820
It’s totally a thing.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.620
A nice reflective metal ruler is basically a diffraction grating.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.760
Feel free to try it at home if you've got one.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:14.380
And thanks for watching!

